#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114922 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "0d6c1aa8-a2d1-5104-8e27-686c7dd16808" :question/math-academy-id "e-3527" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sec x+4=0$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$, what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\sec x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sec x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2\\sec x &= -4 \\\\\n\\sec x &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)}=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nlies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} < 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1565/Source/Images/edfa3375bc3db6b46d1dac36152c42cd.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{8\\pi^2}{9}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114923 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "62e4b12a-19ed-5316-a395-b67da1313687" :question/math-academy-id "e-3529" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x={\\sqrt{2}}$ for $-180^\\circ \\leq x < 180^\\circ$, what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nTherefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) = 45^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1565/Source/Images/1b44825deb488f131a714655b48634f9.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= 360^{∘} - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 360^{∘} - 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 315^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution $x_2$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $360^\\circ$:\n\n$$\nx_2 = 315^\\circ - 360^\\circ = -45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=45^\\circ,-45^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 = 45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114924 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "cc5f8fe7-fd1c-52f9-9dfd-db9ed0a2d973" :question/math-academy-id "e-3532" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi$?\n\n1. $\\sec x = -1$\n2. $\\sec x = 2$\n3. $\\sec x = \\dfrac 1 3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\sec x$ for\n\n$$\n0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$[MATH: \\sec x \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1,\\infty).]$\n\nSo, the equation $\\sec x = k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1)$.\n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114967 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "a3b5986b-17df-5d36-aa1f-6d7d0ef35c85" :question/math-academy-id "e-4260" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x= 3$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$, what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$, rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos (\\frac{1}{3}) = 1.230959… \\approx 1.2310\n$$\n\nSince $1.231\\,0$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 1.231\\,0\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1565/Source/Images/9ea131571a45a21b7339dc32090c32f8.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 2π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 2π - 1.2310 \\\\\n&= 5.0522\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 \\approx 1.231\\,0 \\cdot 5.052\\,2 \\approx 6.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6d89388-dd26-59c2-b62c-aef3350e1be1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113145} {:answer/id #uuid "11d216c9-6d94-57de-b115-a304334c27d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113146} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfd62a7-b472-54ac-a2ee-4b72cf5d625f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113147} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0a8a4e-9945-5e0d-aa15-36e66b5ab94e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113148} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf0683d-0d8a-5267-b81d-502d2a83be7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6d89388-dd26-59c2-b62c-aef3350e1be1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113145} :answer-field/id #uuid "4977aa86-5719-5551-a348-c70c6f677e2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113144}] :question/id #uuid "fcb31a9b-9679-5d24-8bf7-7929bd2e8a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-29163" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0 \\le x < 2π$?\n\n1. $\\sec x = 1$\n2. $\\sec x = 2$\n3. $\\sec x = \\frac{1}{2}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85f71382-728b-